00:21:10,680 --> 00:21:17,560
 So it blocks up and clogs up the tubules that as we talked about with the hypersensitivity

224
00:21:17,560 --> 00:21:23,920
 theory result in that sensitivity that patients often feel.

225
00:21:23,920 --> 00:21:29,400
 This is for if you have two millimeters or more of dentin remaining between the depth

226
00:21:29,400 --> 00:21:32,120
 of the defect and the pulp.

227
00:21:32,120 --> 00:21:34,040
 Gluma is probably the most commonly used.

228
00:21:34,040 --> 00:21:40,560
 It's a combination of glutaraldehyde and hema which is where it gets its name from and

229
00:21:40,560 --> 00:21:43,480
 water as well.

230
00:21:43,480 --> 00:21:49,760
 Liner is used for direct or near pulp exposures so now we're getting closer to encroaching

231
00:21:49,760 --> 00:21:51,320
 on that pulp tissue.

232
00:21:51,320 --> 00:21:57,960
 It provides a barrier to protect dentin from residual reactants, the restoration and oral

233
00:21:57,960 --> 00:21:58,960
 fluids.

234
00:21:58,960 --> 00:22:05,360
 It provides insulation, thermal protection and also popo treatment it can actually cause

235
00:22:05,360 --> 00:22:12,320
 the odonna blast in here to form tertiary reparative dentin.

236
00:22:12,320 --> 00:22:20,240
 And calcium hydroxide or dical is frequently used as a liner and then a base can be used

237
00:22:20,240 --> 00:22:23,960
 for metal restorations and when liner is used.

238
00:22:23,960 --> 00:22:30,920
 So it goes over the liner in order to protect it from being resorbed and washed out.

239
00:22:30,920 --> 00:22:36,760
 The base also provides thermal protection and can distribute local stress across all

240
00:22:36,760 --> 00:22:39,800
 the underlying dentin.

241
00:22:39,800 --> 00:22:45,920
 Resin modified glass ionomer which is what this stands for is frequently used as a base,

242
00:22:45,920 --> 00:22:51,400
 vitre bond is our example here.

243
00:22:51,400 --> 00:22:56,000
 So here's a quick reference chart for what material you're using as your restorative

244
00:22:56,000 --> 00:23:02,480
 material, how far from the pulp you are, which is the remaining dentin thickness.

245
00:23:02,480 --> 00:23:08,560
 That's what that means and what pulp protective materials you might consider using in each

246
00:23:08,560 --> 00:23:10,520
 of those scenarios.

247
00:23:10,520 --> 00:23:15,800
 So feel free to take a screenshot of this if you like for future reference while you're

248
00:23:15,800 --> 00:23:20,360
 studying.

249
00:23:20,360 --> 00:23:27,240
 So now we can think about once all the carries are removed, the pulp is protected if needed.

250
00:23:27,240 --> 00:23:32,640
 We can think about secondary resistance and retention features.

251
00:23:32,640 --> 00:23:36,280
 So these include things like retentive grooves.

252
00:23:36,280 --> 00:23:44,280
 These are little shallow grooves that are placed often with a 169L or a quarter round

253
00:23:44,280 --> 00:23:51,440
 burr just inside the dentin enamel junction to help prevent displacement of the material

254
00:23:51,440 --> 00:23:55,760
 proximally in this case is going to prevent it from moving this way, but it can also be

255
00:23:55,760 --> 00:24:00,120
 placed in areas to prevent occlusal displacement as well.

256
00:24:00,120 --> 00:24:05,440
 It's mostly done for amalgam restorations that don't have that benefit of enamel and

257
00:24:05,440 --> 00:24:08,480
 dentin bonding.

258
00:24:08,480 --> 00:24:15,320
 You can also use beveled enamel margins as seen here and beveled margins provide more

259
00:24:15,320 --> 00:24:24,400
 surface area and roughness for enamel bonding with composite slots and pins can be used

260
00:24:24,400 --> 00:24:28,960
 interchangeably and offer similar amounts of retention.

261
00:24:28,960 --> 00:24:36,320
 Now typically these things are only used for complex amalgam restorations that are either

262
00:24:36,320 --> 00:24:43,880
 missing cusps and/or external walls entirely in order to help bolster their resistance

263
00:24:43,880 --> 00:24:49,400
 and retention form because again with amalgam you cannot rely on bonding.

264
00:24:49,400 --> 00:24:56,240
 So slots we see a slot right here in this complex prep and it has to be at least one

265
00:24:56,240 --> 00:25:05,880
 millimeter deep, one millimeter long and about 0.5 millimeters inside that dentin enamel junction.

266
00:25:05,880 --> 00:25:14,240
 And pins can also be used, self threading are the most common and usually where a vertical

267
00:25:14,240 --> 00:25:16,160
 wall is missing.

268
00:25:16,160 --> 00:25:22,600
 Here is an example of a little pin there that's placed kind of in line where you can imagine

269
00:25:22,600 --> 00:25:29,000
 that cusp tip was at some point before the tooth was prepped.

270
00:25:29,000 --> 00:25:35,360
 So those are some cool tips and tricks to use for adding additional retention and resistance

271
00:25:35,360 --> 00:25:40,880
 form in addition to what you've already done in the initial prep stage, just bolster the

272
00:25:40,880 --> 00:25:44,360
 longevity of that restoration.

273
00:25:44,360 --> 00:25:46,600
 And finally we just want to finish external walls.

274
00:25:46,600 --> 00:25:51,080
 So establish design and smoothness of that capo surface margin.

275
00:25:51,080 --> 00:25:57,560
 We also want to clean and inspect the preparation before progressing to the restoration phase.

276
00:25:57,560 --> 00:26:02,040
 But that's it for the eight steps of the cavity preparation.

277
00:26:02,040 --> 00:26:05,040
 So definitely commit those eight steps to memory.

278
00:26:05,040 --> 00:26:11,080
 This is some really good board exam stuff there.

279
00:26:11,080 --> 00:26:15,720
 The moisture control is something that's being done during the preparation and restoration

280
00:26:15,720 --> 00:26:16,720
 process.

281
00:26:16,720 --> 00:26:21,280
 I've already talked about stuff like this in other series, but you can use rubber dam,

282
00:26:21,280 --> 00:26:27,720
 cotton rolls, dry angle suction, local anesthetic if it has epinephrine, it can also prevent

283
00:26:27,720 --> 00:26:35,080
 some bleeding and prevention of circular fluid, saliva and blood from contaminating where you're

284
00:26:35,080 --> 00:26:41,440
 working is the idea behind these moisture control measures.

285
00:26:41,440 --> 00:26:46,080
 So we're going to finish this video with some specifics concerning preparation design for

286
00:26:46,080 --> 00:26:52,680
 amalgam, composite and gold.

287
00:26:52,680 --> 00:26:59,600
 So we can use for an amalgam preparation any kind of burr that we prefer, but the board

288
00:26:59,600 --> 00:27:06,840
 exam does like to say the carbide burr is preferred because you get these smoother walls.

289
00:27:06,840 --> 00:27:11,160
 For amalgam, we don't really need or want roughened walls.

290
00:27:11,160 --> 00:27:15,240
 Smooth walls are preferable retention.

291
00:27:15,240 --> 00:27:19,840
 We get from occlusal convergence, mostly which we talked about, and then you can also add

292
00:27:19,840 --> 00:27:25,640
 those groove slots or pins for some secondary retention if you like.

293
00:27:25,640 --> 00:27:27,120
 Resistance for the tooth.

294
00:27:27,120 --> 00:27:32,640
 We want to maintain a 90 degree cavosurface margin.

295
00:27:32,640 --> 00:27:34,160
 So what does that mean exactly?

296
00:27:34,160 --> 00:27:42,000
 That means that this tooth surface and this part of the cavosurface margin form a 90 degree

297
00:27:42,000 --> 00:27:43,920
 angle.

298
00:27:43,920 --> 00:27:49,960
 So here and here should be 90 degree angles for the best resistance, the least likelihood

299
00:27:49,960 --> 00:27:56,320
 of fracture of that tooth surface is to maintain that 90 degree cavosurface margin.

300
00:27:56,320 --> 00:27:58,600
 That's really, really helpful.

301
00:27:58,600 --> 00:28:02,640
 We also want to maintain cusps and marginal ridges.

302
00:28:02,640 --> 00:28:06,320
 Those are the parts of the tooth that offer the most strength.

303
00:28:06,320 --> 00:28:11,720
 We want to remove that unsupported or friable enamel that we talked about before.

304
00:28:11,720 --> 00:28:18,840
 We want to maintain flat, popol and gingival floors, rounded internal line angles, and

305
00:28:18,840 --> 00:28:22,600
 pins can offer secondary resistance as well.

306
00:28:22,600 --> 00:28:25,480
 So that's just a review of all the things we talked about.

307
00:28:25,480 --> 00:28:30,880
 Now in terms of the resistance of the amalgam material, we also want that to be 90 degrees

308
00:28:30,880 --> 00:28:36,320
 and that makes sense because if we have 90 degree angle here, we're naturally going to

309
00:28:36,320 --> 00:28:41,680
 have a 90 degree angle between this part of the cavosurface margin and the amalgam that

310
00:28:41,680 --> 00:28:44,880
 will restore that missing tooth structure.

311
00:28:44,880 --> 00:28:50,080
 So 90 and 90, easy to remember, for the board exam.

312
00:28:50,080 --> 00:28:54,800
 And we didn't talk about this before, but we want at least 1 1/2 to 2 millimeters depth

313
00:28:54,800 --> 00:29:00,560
 of the preparation for adequate restorative thickness of the amalgam.

314
00:29:00,560 --> 00:29:06,260
 Which coincidentally, you'll have this if you're at least 0.2 millimeters inside the

315
00:29:06,260 --> 00:29:10,440
 DEJ because the enamel is at least 2 millimeters thick.

316
00:29:10,440 --> 00:29:15,840
 Now if the amalgam material is too thin, it will likely fracture.

317
00:29:15,840 --> 00:29:23,480
 So that's why you want to have at least this minimal depth of restorative material.

318
00:29:23,480 --> 00:29:30,400
 For the composite preparation, the board exam likes to prefer us to use coarse diamonds in

319
00:29:30,400 --> 00:29:33,240
 order to have rough walls.

320
00:29:33,240 --> 00:29:36,760
 So remember I said before, we want smooth walls for amalgam.

321
00:29:36,760 --> 00:29:43,240
 For composite, the rough walls offer more surface area, which gives us better retention

322
00:29:43,240 --> 00:29:44,980
 when we're bonding.

323
00:29:44,980 --> 00:29:48,880
 So that's why coarse diamonds can be preferred.

324
00:29:48,880 --> 00:29:55,800
 The next thing here is that really all of those philosophies stay true for composite, except

325
00:29:55,800 --> 00:30:00,880
 there's not really a need for those retentive features, those secondary features we talked

326
00:30:00,880 --> 00:30:07,000
 about, grooves, slats, pins, things like that, occlusal convergence isn't as needed, it's

327
00:30:07,000 --> 00:30:12,600
 still a good practice to keep, but it's not as relied upon.

328
00:30:12,600 --> 00:30:15,440
 And we don't need as much of a uniform depth.

329
00:30:15,440 --> 00:30:20,360
 So the basic idea here is there's a lot, a little bit more freedom, and we can perform

330
00:30:20,360 --> 00:30:27,040
 what's called a conservative prep design and save more to structure as opposed to a strictly

331
00:30:27,040 --> 00:30:36,800
 conventional prep design that focuses very strictly on retention and resistance features.

332
00:30:36,800 --> 00:30:39,200
 And lastly, the gold onlay preparation.

333
00:30:39,200 --> 00:30:44,320
 I'm not going to go into too much depth here, because this gets really convoluted, but the

334
00:30:44,320 --> 00:30:49,200
 most important things to know for the board exam are what these terms mean.

335
00:30:49,200 --> 00:30:55,160
 So again, the gold onlay preparation can get very, very complex, but if you can just think

336
00:30:55,160 --> 00:31:02,360
 of these couple quick, these nice, what do you call them, buzzwords we'll call them for

337
00:31:02,360 --> 00:31:06,120
 the board exam, because sometimes these words show up and it's just kind of nice to know

338
00:31:06,120 --> 00:31:07,120
 what they might mean.

339
00:31:07,120 --> 00:31:14,000
 So a collar is talking about this beveled shoulder around a capped cusp for in order

340
00:31:14,000 --> 00:31:21,600
 to offer resistance form, this bracing effect, and a skirt is an even less aggressive and

341
00:31:21,600 --> 00:31:27,240
 a mini preparation around a capped cusp with like a feather edge margin.

342
00:31:27,240 --> 00:31:33,400
 And both of these things provide secondary resistance and retention form for your gold

343
00:31:33,400 --> 00:31:37,000
 onlay preparation.

